<h2>What may have survived</h2>
<p>For everyone who fought in 1941–1945 there may be a draft office registration card, a unit's loss report ("killed", "missing in action"), an award sheet and order, hospital records, a prisoner-of-war card, a burial record. Most of these are held by the Central Archive of the Russian Ministry of Defence (TsAMO, Podolsk) and have been scanned into open databases. The sites are in Russian; type the name in Cyrillic.</p>

<h2>How to search</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Start with Pamyat Naroda.</strong> Search by surname, first name, patronymic and year of birth. It brings together OBD Memorial and Podvig Naroda, though not always completely — check those too.</li>
  <li><strong>Try other spellings.</strong> Clerks mixed up letters, shortened names ("Ив."), got the patronymic or the birth year wrong by a year or two. Search without the patronymic, without the year, with one letter of the surname changed.</li>
  <li><strong>Open the document itself, not just the card.</strong> A loss report may give the place of enlistment, the family's address, the name of a wife or mother. That is what tells your ancestor apart from a namesake.</li>
  <li><strong>Find the unit.</strong> Pamyat Naroda shows a unit's combat path by its number; the index of Red Army units decodes a field post number from a letter.</li>
  <li><strong>Captivity and forced labour.</strong> If the person was a prisoner of war or taken to Germany for labour, search Arolsen Archives.</li>
</ol>

<h2>The First World War</h2>
<p>For soldiers of 1914–1918 there is Heroes of the Great War: loss cards (wounded, killed, missing, captured), hospitals, awards. Search by surname and by place — governorate, uyezd, volost, village. The village on a card is often garbled: compare it with other cards from the same volost and with old lists of settlements rather than picking a village that merely sounds alike.</p>

<h2>What to keep in the tree</h2>
<p>The database, the link to the document, the archive and reference (for example "TsAMO, f. 58, op. 18001, d. 123") and what the document says, word for word. A matching name is not proof yet: note what matched (birth year, place of enlistment, a wife's name) and what didn't.</p>
